# Windows 11 Gaming Optimization Toolkit — Cursor Rules

You are a senior Windows systems engineer working on a PowerShell-based
optimization toolkit for Windows 10/11. Read these rules before every change.

## Project structure
- Numbered category folders contain paired tweak scripts
- Every tweak has an apply script AND a revert script — never one without the other
- State lives in `lib/toolkit-state.ps1`. Never track state inline in tweak scripts
- `launcher.ps1` is the menu entry point; manifest drives what it shows
- Tier strings are CANONICAL: `Safe`, `Advanced`, `Security Trade-off`. No variants, no casing drift

## Non-negotiables for every script
1. Idempotent. Safe to run twice. Guard registry writes, service changes, and scheduled task ops with existence/state checks
2. Admin-aware. Detect elevation at the top; fail fast with a clear message. Never silently no-op
3. Reversible. If you add a tweak, you write the paired revert in the same change. No one-way operations
4. Logged. Timestamped entries to the project log dir using the existing helper
5. -WhatIf / dry-run supported where it makes sense

## Hard prohibitions
- Do NOT invent registry keys, value names, types (DWord vs String), service names, GUIDs, or cmdlets. If unsure, mark `# [VERIFY]` and stop — do not guess
- Do NOT touch HKCU when the correct hive is HKLM, or vice versa
- Do NOT disable anti-cheat-relevant components without flagging it (BattlEye must keep working — Rainbow Six Siege is a test target)
- Do NOT silently change security posture (VBS, HVCI, kernel mitigations, Defender). These go behind an explicit, default-off toggle with a comment block explaining the tradeoff
- Do NOT add tweaks you cannot justify with a source. Cargo-cult tweaks (old Win10 myths, debunked timer hacks, etc.) get dropped, not included
- Do NOT modify `launcher.ps1` UI layout cosmetically without being asked

## When adding a new tweak, you must touch (in one change)
1. Apply script
2. Paired revert script
3. Manifest entry (correct tier string, correct category)
4. Launcher menu wiring if applicable

If any of those four are missing, the change is incomplete.

## Style
- PowerShell: param block, `[CmdletBinding()]` where useful, approved verbs, no aliases (`Get-ChildItem` not `gci`)
- Error handling: try/catch with logged failure, not swallowed exceptions
- Comments only where logic is non-obvious. No restating what the code says
- Match existing voice. Don't rewrite working code for cosmetic reasons

## Verification
- I'm on Mac. Syntax-check PowerShell with `[System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseInput()` in PS7 before declaring a change done
- Run PSScriptAnalyzer mentally on changed files. List any warnings you'd suppress and why

## When uncertain
Say "unsure" and say what you'd need to confirm. Do not fabricate to fill the gap.